            <normalization>
               <p>All the abbreviations used by the scribe have been resolved.</p>
               <p>Chabot's edition does not distinguish between diacritical points above and below letters and vowels, such as pṯāḥā. For this reason, all of these have been represented as diacritical points, as we have no direct access to the manuscript itself.</p>
            </normalization>
            <punctuation>
               <p>The punctuation follows that found in Chabot's edition. The diacritical dot placed above the taw on third feminine singular perfect verbs has been indicated. This is also the case for nouns ending in taw in the construct state that make use of the same diacritical dot.</p>
            </punctuation>
